WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.106
A Bitcoin proposal can have a plausible fee and still pay the wrong person.

00:05.106 --> 00:10.212
SatsGuard compares a partially signed Bitcoin transaction with the payment you meant to make.

00:10.212 --> 00:13.129
This AI-assisted prototype was built for Prakhar Singh.

00:13.129 --> 00:17.611
These are actual app captures using synthetic testnet fixtures.

00:17.611 --> 00:23.860
The policy declares the network, recipients, exact satoshi amounts, allowed change addresses, and an absolute fee cap.

00:23.860 --> 00:27.903
You can paste or load a base sixty-four or hex PSBT.

00:27.903 --> 00:30.843
Change is accepted only when you declare it.

00:30.843 --> 00:36.086
The app does not guess which outputs belong to your wallet.

00:36.086 --> 00:40.674
In the recipient-swap example, the claimed fee is only one thousand sats.

00:40.674 --> 00:44.116
But fifty thousand sats go to an undeclared destination.

00:44.116 --> 00:49.851
SatsGuard marks that output and reports a payment amount mismatch: the intended recipient receives zero.

00:49.851 --> 00:56.786
The allocation map makes this difference visible instead of burying it in raw transaction data.

00:56.786 --> 00:58.678
Now compare the matched example.

00:58.678 --> 01:03.216
The same supplied one hundred thousand sats become fifty thousand for the

01:03.216 --> 01:07.377
declared payment, forty-nine thousand for declared change, and a one-thousand-sat fee.

01:07.377 --> 01:10.781
No declared checks failed means the policy comparison passed.

01:10.781 --> 01:15.763
It does not mean the transaction is safe to sign.

01:15.763 --> 01:18.843
The fee-spike fixture still pays the expected recipient.

01:18.843 --> 01:24.619
But its supplied input and output values imply a ten-thousand-sat fee, above the two-thousand-sat cap.

01:24.619 --> 01:26.929
The report fails the fee check.

01:26.929 --> 01:35.060
Accounting uses whole satoshis and supplied PSBT values, with the source of those values stated in the report.

01:35.060 --> 01:40.260
Remove the supplied input value and the recipients still match, but the fee becomes unknown.

01:40.260 --> 01:43.727
The app requests review because it cannot check the cap.

01:43.727 --> 01:47.888
This example was also rerun with the browser network disabled after loading.

01:47.888 --> 01:52.901
Analysis stays local and makes no chain lookup or payload upload.

01:52.901 --> 01:56.541
The command-line interface runs the same deterministic engine.

01:56.541 --> 02:03.367
Here is an actual recipient-swap run: blocked status, a one-thousand-sat fee, and one unexpected output.

02:03.367 --> 02:08.827
The JSON export excludes addresses, scripts, outpoints, public keys, and raw payloads.

02:08.827 --> 02:14.288
Amounts and transaction shape remain, so redaction is not an anonymity guarantee.

02:14.288 --> 02:17.763
No AI model is integrated.

02:17.763 --> 02:24.381
SatsGuard is a small PSBT version-zero preflight prototype, not a wallet or an audited security system.

02:24.381 --> 02:28.517
It does not verify signatures, ownership, spendability, or chain state.

02:28.517 --> 02:31.826
It never asks for keys, signs, or broadcasts.

02:31.826 --> 02:39.644
The goal is a reviewable comparison between declared intent and the proposal in front of you.
